.TH KEYBOARD.H 3v32 "October 14, 2026" "v32io 20261014-dev" "Vircon32 C Library"
.SH NAME
keyboard.h \- v32kbd: read a keyboard through a gamepad port
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include \(dqkeyboard.h\(dq
.PP
.BI "v32kbd* v32kbd_init( int " gamepad " );"
.BI "void    v32kbd_free( v32kbd** " keyboard " );"
.PP
.BI "bool    v32kbd_probe( v32kbd** " keyboard " );"
.BI "int     v32kbd_read( v32kbd** " keyboard " );"
.BI "int     v32kbd_readevent( v32kbd** " keyboard ", bool* " pressed " );"
.BI "int     v32kbd_glyph( int " value " );"
.BI "bool    v32kbd_isdown( v32kbd** " keyboard ", int " key " );"
.PP
.BI "v32key* v32kbd_getkey( v32kbd** " keyboard " );"
.BI "int     v32kbd_symbol( v32kbd* " keyboard ", int " key " );"
.BI "int     v32kbd_addkey( v32kbd** " keyboard ", v32key* " key " );"
.BI "v32key* v32key_newkey( int " key " );"
.BI "int     v32kbd_scan( v32kbd* " keyboard " );"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A v32kbd is a keyboard that presents itself to the console as a gamepad
(see
.BR v32io.h (3v32)).
The emulator provides one with the host's keyboard, and the RP2350-USB-A
adapter provides one with a real USB keyboard. This header decodes it into
key events, and keeps the state of the keys and of caps lock, so a program
can read typed text or poll keys like a game.
.PP
The device reports
.B one event per frame
at the most, and holds it until the next one. The Left and Right controls
are a strobe that switches side with every new event, so an event is
recognized as new when the side differs from the last seen. The other
controls carry the event:
.PP
.nf
Left / Right  strobe (Left first)
Up            the key was pressed
Down          the key was released
Start, A, B, X, Y, L, R   key code, bits 0 to 6
.fi
.PP
The program calls
.B v32kbd_probe
.B once on every frame
for each keyboard. Events that arrive faster than they are read wait in a
queue of up to
.B V32KBD_MAXQUEUE
(64); while it is full new events are dropped.
.SS Key codes
A key code identifies a
.I key
(shift is not part of it). Keys with an ASCII character use it as their code,
as typed on a US layout with no shift:
.BR a " to " z ,
.BR 0 " to " 9 ,
space, and
.B "` \- = [ ] \e ; ' , . /"
The other keys have these codes:
.PP
.nf
V32KEY_NONE       0       V32KEY_ENTER      13
V32KEY_UP         1       V32KEY_F1         14   (F1 to F12 are 14 to 25)
V32KEY_DOWN       2       V32KEY_F12        25
V32KEY_LEFT       3       V32KEY_RALT       26
V32KEY_RIGHT      4       V32KEY_ESCAPE     27
V32KEY_CAPSLOCK   5       V32KEY_LGUI       28
V32KEY_LSHIFT     6       V32KEY_RGUI       29
V32KEY_RSHIFT     7       V32KEY_PAGE_HIGH  30   (see below)
V32KEY_BACKSPACE  8       V32KEY_PAGE_LOW   31   (see below)
V32KEY_TAB        9       V32KEY_DELETE    127
V32KEY_LCTRL     10
V32KEY_RCTRL     11
V32KEY_LALT      12
.fi
.PP
LALT, RALT, LGUI and RGUI are the Option and Command keys on a Mac.
.SS Typed characters
The key code of a key cannot say what the key typed when that is not
ASCII: an accented letter, a euro sign, a box drawing character. For
these the device can send the
.I character
itself, in the character set of the BIOS font (Windows\-1252, with
box drawing characters in the place of codes 14 to 31).
.PP
A character takes two consecutive events. The first is a
.IR "codepage prefix" ,
one of the two key codes that were left unused; the second, the
.IR payload ,
carries the low 7 bits of the character in its key code field (0 is a valid
payload):
.PP
.nf
V32KEY_PAGE_HIGH + payload   character 128 + payload   (128 to 255)
V32KEY_PAGE_LOW  + payload   character payload         (0 to 127)
.fi
.PP
Both events are sent as key
.I releases
so a program built with a library from before this extension sees two
key releases (which its
.B v32kbd_read
skips) instead of a key press. This library takes either action.
A prefix that is not followed by its payload within
.B V32KBD_PAGE_TIMEOUT
(30) frames is forgotten.
.PP
Typed characters appear in the queue as events with the
.I text
flag set. They are returned by
.B v32kbd_read
and
.BR v32kbd_getkey ,
and skipped by
.BR v32kbd_readevent ,
because they are not keys. On a character sent as text the key that typed it
is not reported at all.
.SS Which characters
ASCII characters come from the keys as always, with
.B v32kbd_symbol
applying shift and caps lock for a US layout. The emulator additionally
reports whatever the host's own keyboard layout types, if the BIOS font has
the character. The RP2350\-USB\-A adapter, which only sees which keys are
pressed, has its own way of typing them: AltGr and dead keys, and Alt codes
on the numeric keypad (see
.I docs/COMPOSE.md
in the v32io project).
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.BI "v32kbd_init( int " gamepad )
Create a keyboard on the given gamepad port (see the port ids in
.BR v32io.h (3v32)),
or return
.B NULL
if memory runs out. An event the device was holding at that moment is not
read as new.
.TP
.BI "v32kbd_free( v32kbd** " keyboard )
Release a keyboard and any events waiting in it, and set the pointer to
.BR NULL .
.TP
.BI "v32kbd_probe( v32kbd** " keyboard )
Check for input. Returns true if an event or a typed character was received
in this frame.
.TP
.BI "v32kbd_read( v32kbd** " keyboard )
The next key
.IR press ,
skipping releases, or 0 if nothing is left. The value is the
.I symbol
of the key: for a key with an ASCII character, the character with shift
and caps lock applied (so \(aqA\(aq, \(aq!\(aq...); for any other key its key code. A typed character
is returned as its BIOS font code (128 to 255, or 32 to 126), and a typed character
below 32 as that code plus
.B V32KBD_GLYPH
(256), so it cannot be confused with a key code. Pass the value to
.B v32kbd_glyph
to know what to draw.
.TP
.BI "v32kbd_glyph( int " value )
For a value returned by
.BR v32kbd_read ,
the BIOS font character to draw, or 0 if it is not a character. That is the
case for keys with no character (arrows, F keys, Escape, Delete, Backspace,
Enter, Tab...) and for 0. Values 32 to 126, and those from typed characters, are
characters; so are 128 to 255. This is the function that tells a box
drawing character (value 256 + code) from a key with the same code.
.TP
.BI "v32kbd_readevent( v32kbd** " keyboard ", bool* " pressed )
The next key event, press or release, as its key code (not the symbol), or
0 if there is none. If
.I pressed
is not
.B NULL
it is set to true for a press. Typed characters are skipped. Use this for
games that need to know when a key is released.
.TP
.BI "v32kbd_isdown( v32kbd** " keyboard ", int " key )
True while the key with that code is held (keys are tracked as the events
arrive, not as they are read). For modifiers and game controls.
.TP
.BI "v32kbd_getkey( v32kbd** " keyboard )
Take the next
.I v32key
from the queue, or return
.B NULL
if it is empty. The caller must
.B free
it. The v32key has everything about the event: use it to read typed
characters apart from keys.
.TP
.BI "v32kbd_symbol( v32kbd* " keyboard ", int " key )
The character a key code types with the current state of shift and caps
lock (US layout): letters invert with caps lock, numbers and punctuation
follow shift only. Keys that have no character return their key code.
.TP
.BI "v32kbd_addkey( v32kbd** " keyboard ", v32key* " key )
Append a key event to the queue. Returns 1 on success. Used by
.BR v32kbd_probe ;
a program can use it to inject events.
.TP
.BI "v32key_newkey( int " key )
Allocate a v32key for the key code: a press, with the symbol equal to the code,
and not text.
.TP
.BI "v32kbd_scan( v32kbd* " keyboard )
Read the packed controls of the keyboard's gamepad right away
(see
.BR v32io_scan ).
Kept for compatibility.
.SH VARIABLES
.TP
.B struct v32key
One event in the queue.
.nf
int     value;    key code; for text, the BIOS font code
int     symbol;   typed character with shift applied
bool    pressed   true: pressed, false: released
bool    text;     true: a typed character, not a key
v32key* next;
.fi
.TP
.B struct v32kbd
A keyboard instance. A program should not need to touch its fields; they are
.IR io ", " input ", " data ", " count ", " gamepad ", " lastread ", " strobe ,
.IR page ", " pageframe ", " capslock " and the table " held .
.TP
.BR V32KBD_STROBE ", " V32KBD_PRESSED ", " V32KBD_RELEASED
Masks of the strobe (3), the press (4) and the release (8) controls in the
packed controls.
.TP
.BR V32KBD_CODE_SHIFT ", " V32KBD_CODE_MASK
The key code is at bit 4 of the packed controls (4) and is 7 bits long
(127).
.TP
.B V32KBD_KEYS
128, the number of key codes.
.TP
.B V32KBD_MAXQUEUE
64, events that can wait to be read.
.TP
.B V32KBD_GLYPH
256, added by
.B v32kbd_read
to typed characters below 32.
.TP
.B V32KBD_PAGE_TIMEOUT
30, frames a codepage prefix waits for its payload.
.SH EXAMPLES
Collect typed text on a line, accented letters included, and draw it.
.B print_at
draws each value of the text as the BIOS font character with that code, so the
glyphs from
.B v32kbd_glyph
can be stored in it as they are.
.PP
.RS
.nf
#include "video.h"
#include "keyboard.h"

void main()
{
    v32kbd* keyboard = v32kbd_init( SECOND_GAMEPAD_PORT );
    int [100] line;
    int length = 0;
    int key = 0;
    int glyph = 0;

    line[ 0 ] = 0;

    while( true )
    {
        v32kbd_probe( &keyboard );

        key = v32kbd_read( &keyboard );
        while( key > 0 )
        {
            glyph = v32kbd_glyph( key );

            if( glyph > 0 && length < 98 )
            {
                line[ length ] = glyph;
                length = length + 1;
                line[ length ] = 0;
            }
            else if( key == V32KEY_BACKSPACE && length > 0 )
            {
                length = length - 1;
                line[ length ] = 0;
            }

            key = v32kbd_read( &keyboard );
        }

        clear_screen( color_black );
        print_at( 10, 10, line );
        end_frame();
    }
}
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
Call
.B v32kbd_probe
on every frame; a skipped frame can lose an event. A keyboard on a gamepad
port that is disconnected and connected again starts over: pending events and
a waiting prefix are forgotten.
.PP
The keyboard is a gamepad, so a gamepad on the same port cannot be used at
the same time. The emulator uses the host's keyboard for either its normal
keyboard gamepad or v32kbd, but not for both.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR v32io.h (3v32),
.BR mouse.h (3v32),
.BR v32io (3v32),
.BR input.h (3v32),
.BR video.h (3v32)
.SH AUTHOR
Matthew Haas, for the v32io project. The Vircon32 standard library it builds
on was written by Javier Carracedo
.RI ( Carra ).
